4
00:00:12,960 --> 00:00:16,480
Today's reading by Alex Foster.

5
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:19,600
Frankenstein by Mary Shelley.

6
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:24,240
Chapter 8.

7
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:29,120
We passed a few sad hours until eleven o'clock, when the trial was to commence.

8
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:35,200
My father and the rest of the family being obliged to attend as witnesses, I accompanied them to the court.

9
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:40,240
During the whole of this wretched mockery of justice, I suffered living torture.

10
00:00:40,240 --> 00:01:00,240
It was to be decided whether the result of my curiosity and lawless devices would cause the death of two of my fellow beings, one a smiling babe full of innocence and joy, the other far more dreadfully murdered, with every aggravation of infamy that could make the murder memorable in horror.

11
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:06,000
Justine also was a girl of merit and possessed qualities which promised to render her life happy.

12
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:12,800
Now all was to be obliterated in an ignominious grave, and I the cause.

13
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:18,880
A thousand times rather would I have confessed myself guilty of the crime ascribed to Justine.

14
00:01:18,880 --> 00:01:29,840
But I was absent when it was committed, and such a declaration would have been considered as the ravings of a madman, and would not have exculpated her who suffered through me.

15
00:01:29,840 --> 00:01:32,640
The appearance of Justine was calm.

16
00:01:32,640 --> 00:01:41,280
She was dressed in mourning, and her countenance, always engaging, was rendered by the solemnity of her feelings exquisitely beautiful.

17
00:01:41,280 --> 00:01:58,080
Yet she appeared confident in innocence and did not tremble, although gazed on and execrated by thousands for all the kindness which her beauty might otherwise have excited, was obliterated in the minds of the spectators by the imagination of the enormity she was supposed to have committed.

18
00:01:58,080 --> 00:02:11,080
She was tranquil, yet her tranquility was evidently constrained, and as her confusion had before her been adduced as proof of her guilt, she worked up in her mind to an appearance of courage.

19
00:02:11,080 --> 00:02:17,080
When she entered the court, she threw her eyes round it and quickly discovered where we were seated.

20
00:02:17,080 --> 00:02:27,480
A tear seemed to dim her eye when she saw us, but she quickly recovered herself, and a look of sorrowful affection seemed to attest her utter guiltlessness.

21
00:02:27,480 --> 00:02:34,360
The trial began, and after the advocate against her had stated the charge, several witnesses were called.

22
00:02:34,360 --> 00:02:42,120
Several strange facts combined against her, which might have staggered anyone who had not such proof of her innocence as I had.

23
00:02:42,120 --> 00:02:53,640
She had been out the whole of the night on which the murder had been committed, and towards morning had been perceived by a market woman not far from the spot where the body of the murdered child had been afterwards found.

24
00:02:53,640 --> 00:03:01,640
The woman asked her what she did there, but she looked very strangely and only returned a confused and unintelligible answer.

25
00:03:01,640 --> 00:03:13,880
She returned to the house about eight o'clock, and when one inquired where she had passed the night, she replied that she had been looking for the child and demanded earnestly if anything had been heard concerning him.

26
00:03:13,880 --> 00:03:19,640
When shown the body, she fell into violent hysterics and kept her bed for several days.

27
00:03:19,640 --> 00:03:33,720
The picture was then produced, which the servant had found in her pocket, and when Elizabeth, in a faltering voice, proved that it was the same which, an hour before the child had been missed, she had placed around his neck.

28
00:03:33,720 --> 00:03:38,600
A murmur of horror and indignation filled the court.

29
00:03:38,600 --> 00:03:41,720
Justine was called on for her defence.

30
00:03:41,720 --> 00:03:46,320
As the trial had proceeded, her countenance had halted.

31
00:03:44,840 --> 00:03:50,480
Surprise, horror, and misery were strongly expressed.

32
00:03:50,880 --> 00:04:04,000
Sometimes she struggled with her tears, but when she was desired to plead, she collected her powers and spoke in an audible though variable voice.

33
00:04:04,240 --> 00:04:13,040
God knows, she said, how entirely I am innocent, but I do not pretend that my protestations should acquit me.

34
00:04:13,040 --> 00:04:30,000
I rest my innocence on a plain and simple explanation of the facts which have been adduced against me, and I hope the character I have always borne will incline my judges to a favorable interpretation, where any circumstance appears doubtful or suspicious.

35
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:42,720
She then related that by the permission of Elizabeth, she had passed the evening of the night on which the murder had been committed at the house of an aunt at Schen, a village situated about a league from Geneva.

36
00:04:42,720 --> 00:04:49,920
On her return, at about nine o'clock, she met a man who asked her if she had seen anything of the child who was lost.

37
00:04:49,920 --> 00:05:04,400
She was alarmed by this account and passed several hours in looking for him when the gates of Geneva were shut, and she was forced to remain several hours of the night in a barn belonging to a cottage, being unwilling to call up the inhabitants to whom she was well known.

38
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:06,960
Most of the night she spent here watching.

39
00:05:06,960 --> 00:05:10,240
Towards morning, she believed that she slept for a few minutes.

40
00:05:10,240 --> 00:05:13,120
Some steps disturbed her, and she awoke.

41
00:05:13,120 --> 00:05:19,200
It was dawn, and she quitted her asylum that she might again endeavour to find my brother.

42
00:05:19,200 --> 00:05:24,480
If she had gone near the spot where his body lay, it was without her knowledge.

43
00:05:24,480 --> 00:05:33,640
That she had been bewildered when questioned by the marketwoman was not surprising, since she had passed a sleepless night, and the fate of poor William was yet uncertain.

44
00:05:34,120 --> 00:05:38,440
Concerning the picture, she could give no account.

45
00:05:38,600 --> 00:05:57,560
I know, continued the unhappy victim, how heavily and fatally this one circumstance weighs against me, but I have no power of explaining it, and when I have expressed my utter ignorance, I am only left to conjecture concerning the probabilities by which it might have been placed in my pocket.

46
00:05:57,560 --> 00:05:59,560
But here also I am checked.

47
00:05:59,560 --> 00:06:06,280
I believe that I have no enemy on earth, and none surely would have been so wicked as to destroy me wantonly.

48
00:06:06,280 --> 00:06:08,520
Did the murderer place it there?

49
00:06:08,520 --> 00:06:19,400
I know of no opportunity afforded him for so doing, or if I had, why should he have stolen the jewel to part with it again so soon?

50
00:06:19,400 --> 00:06:25,560
I commit my cause to the justice of my judges, yet I see no room for hope.

51
00:06:25,560 --> 00:06:41,480
I beg permission to have a few witnesses examined concerning my character, and if their testimony shall not overweigh my supposed guilt, I must be condemned, although I would pledge my salvation on my innocence.

52
00:06:41,480 --> 00:06:46,840
Several witnesses were called who had known her for many years, and they spoke well of her.

53
00:06:46,840 --> 00:06:54,600
But fear and hatred of the crime of which they supposed her guilty rendered them timorous and unwilling to come forward.

54
00:06:54,600 --> 00:07:08,280
Elizabeth saw even this last resource, her excellent dispositions and irreproachable conduct about to fail the accused, when, although violently agitated, she desired permission to address the court.

55
00:07:08,440 --> 00:07:21,280
I am said she, the cousin of the unhappy child who was murdered, or rather his sister, for I was educated by and have lived with his parents ever since and even long before his birth.

56
00:07:21,520 --> 00:07:37,040
It may therefore be judged indecent in me to come forward on this occasion, but when I see a fellow creature about to perish due the cowardice of her pretended friends, I wish to be allowed to speak that I may say what I know of her character.

57
00:07:37,040 --> 00:07:39,760
I am well acquainted with the accused.

58
00:07:39,760 --> 00:07:46,400
I have lived in the same house with her at one time for five and at another for nearly two years.

59
00:07:46,400 --> 00:07:52,880
During all that period, she appeared to me the most amiable and benevolent of human creatures.

60
00:07:52,880 --> 00:08:06,720
She nursed Madame Frankenstein, my aunt in her last illness, with the greatest affection and care, and afterwards attended her own mother during a tedious illness, in a manner that excited the admiration of all who knew her.

61
00:08:06,720 --> 00:08:12,080
After which she again lived in my uncle's house, where she was beloved by all the family.

62
00:08:12,080 --> 00:08:18,720
She was warmly attached to the child who is now dead, and acted towards him like a most affectionate mother.

63
00:08:18,720 --> 00:08:28,720
For my own part, I do not hesitate to say that, notwithstanding all the evidence produced against her, I believe and rely on her perfect innocence.

64
00:08:28,720 --> 00:08:31,760
She had no temptation for such an action.

65
00:08:31,760 --> 00:08:43,280
As to the bauble on which the chief proof rests, if she had so earnestly desired it, I should have willingly given it to her, so much do I esteem and value her.

66
00:08:45,160 --> 00:08:59,800
Followed Elizabeth's simple and powerful appeal, but it was excited by her generous interference, and not in favour of poor Justine, on whom the public indignation was turned with renewed violence, charging her with the blackest ingratitude.

67
00:08:59,800 --> 00:09:04,920
She herself wept as Elizabeth spoke, but she did not answer.

68
00:09:04,920 --> 00:09:09,320
My own agitation and anguish was extreme during the whole trial.

69
00:09:09,320 --> 00:09:12,520
I believed in her innocence, I knew it.

70
00:09:12,520 --> 00:09:21,560
Could the demon who had, I did not a minute doubt, murdered my brother also in his hellish sport, have betrayed the innocent to death and ignominy.

71
00:09:21,560 --> 00:09:34,360
I could not sustain the horror of my situation, and when I perceived that the popular voice and the countenances of the judge had already condemned my unhappy victim, I rushed out of the court in agony.

72
00:09:34,360 --> 00:09:37,960
The tortures of the accused did not equal mine.

73
00:09:37,960 --> 00:09:46,360
She was sustained by innocence, but the fangs of remorse tore my bosom and would not forgo their hold.

74
00:09:46,360 --> 00:09:50,200
I passed the night of unmingled wretchedness.

75
00:09:50,200 --> 00:09:52,680
In the morning I went to the court.

76
00:09:52,680 --> 00:09:55,400
My lips and throat were parched.

77
00:09:55,400 --> 00:10:01,720
I dared not ask the fatal question, but I was known, and the officer guessed the cause of my visit.

78
00:10:01,720 --> 00:10:08,440
The ballots had been thrown, they were all black, and Justine was condemned.

79
00:10:08,440 --> 00:10:12,760
I cannot pretend to describe what I then felt.

80
00:10:12,760 --> 00:10:25,880
I had before experienced sensations of horror, and when I have endeavoured to bestow upon them adequate expressions, but words cannot convey an idea of the heart-sickening despair that I then endured.

81
00:10:25,880 --> 00:10:32,040
The person to whom I addressed myself added that Justine had already confessed her guilt.

82
00:10:32,040 --> 00:10:45,280
That evidence, he observed, was hardly required in so glaring a case, but I am glad of it, and indeed, none of our judges like to condemn a criminal upon circumstantial evidence, be it ever so decisive.

83
00:10:44,360 --> 00:10:48,480
This was strange and unexpected intelligence.

84
00:10:48,720 --> 00:10:50,480
What could it mean?

85
00:10:50,480 --> 00:10:58,720
Had my eyes deceived me, and was I really as mad as the whole world would believe me to be if I disclosed the object of my suspicions.

86
00:10:58,720 --> 00:11:04,080
I hastened to return home, and Elizabeth eagerly demanded the result.

87
00:11:04,240 --> 00:11:10,160
My cousin, replied I, it is decided as you may have expected.

88
00:11:10,160 --> 00:11:16,400
All judges had rather that ten innocent should suffer than that one guilty should escape.

89
00:11:16,400 --> 00:11:25,360
But she has confessed.

90
00:11:25,360 --> 00:11:32,000
This was a dire blow to poor Elizabeth, who had relied with firmness upon Justine's innocence.

91
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:36,400
Alas, said she, how shall I ever again believe in human goodness?

92
00:11:36,400 --> 00:11:43,360
Justine, whom I loved and esteemed as my sister, how could she put on those smiles of innocence only to betray?

93
00:11:43,360 --> 00:11:50,880
Her mild eyes seemed incapable of any severity or guile, and yet she has committed a murder.

94
00:11:50,880 --> 00:11:56,320
Soon after we heard that the poor victim had expressed a desire to see my cousin.

95
00:11:56,320 --> 00:12:02,560
My father wished her not to go, but said that he left it to her own judgment and feelings to decide.

96
00:12:02,560 --> 00:12:09,520
Yes, said Elizabeth, I will go, although she is guilty, and you, Victor, shall accompany me.

97
00:12:09,520 --> 00:12:12,240
I cannot go alone.

98
00:12:12,240 --> 00:12:17,360
The idea of this visit was torture to me, yet I could not refuse.

99
00:12:17,360 --> 00:12:23,120
We entered the gloomy prison chamber and beheld Justine sitting on some straw at the farther end.

100
00:12:23,120 --> 00:12:27,360
Her hands were manacled, and her head rested on her knees.

101
00:12:27,360 --> 00:12:35,080
She rose on seeing us enter, and when we were left alone with her, she threw herself at the feet of Elizabeth, weeping bitterly.

102
00:12:35,400 --> 00:12:38,120
My cousin wept also.

103
00:12:43,640 --> 00:12:51,960
I relied on your innocence, and although I was then very wretched, I was not so miserable as I am now.

104
00:12:51,960 --> 00:12:55,640
And do you also believe that I am so very, very wicked?

105
00:12:55,640 --> 00:13:00,280
Do you also join with my enemies to crush me, to condemn me as a murderer?

106
00:13:00,280 --> 00:13:03,880
Her voice was suffocated with sobs.

107
00:13:05,560 --> 00:13:09,160
Elizabeth, why do you kneel if you are innocent?

108
00:13:09,160 --> 00:13:11,080
I am not one of your enemies.

109
00:13:11,080 --> 00:13:18,200
I believed you guiltless, notwithstanding every evidence, until I heard that you had yourself declared your guilt.

110
00:13:18,200 --> 00:13:28,200
That report you say is false, and be assured, dear Justine, that nothing can shake my confidence in you for a moment, but your own confession.

111
00:13:28,200 --> 00:13:32,040
I did confess, but I confessed a lie.

112
00:13:32,040 --> 00:13:40,040
I confessed that I might obtain absolution, but now that falsehood lies heavier at my heart than all my other sins.

113
00:13:40,040 --> 00:13:42,520
The God of heaven forgive me.

114
00:13:42,520 --> 00:13:46,200
Ever since I was condemned, my confessor has besieged me.

115
00:13:46,200 --> 00:13:51,800
He threatened and menaced until I almost began to think that I was the monster that he said I was.

116
00:13:51,800 --> 00:13:57,640
He threatened excommunication and hellfire in my last moments if I continued obdurate.

117
00:13:57,640 --> 00:14:00,360
Dear lady, I I had none to support me.

118
00:14:00,520 --> 00:14:05,000
All looked on me as a wretch, doomed to ignominy and perition.

119
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:06,600
What could I do?

120
00:14:06,600 --> 00:14:13,160
In an evil hour, I subscribed to a lie, and now only am I truly miserable.

121
00:14:13,160 --> 00:14:16,800
She paused, weeping, and then continued.

122
00:14:17,680 --> 00:14:31,280
I thought with horror, my sweet lady, that you should believe your Justine, whom your blessed aunt had so highly honored and whom you loved was a creature capable of a crime which none but the devil himself could have perpetrated.

123
00:14:31,280 --> 00:14:46,240
Dear William, dearest blessed child, I soon shall see you again in heaven, where we shall all be happy, and that consoles me, going as I am to suffer ignominy and death.

124
00:14:46,240 --> 00:14:51,120
Oh Justine, forgive me for having for one moment distrusted you.

125
00:14:51,120 --> 00:14:53,440
Why did you confess?

126
00:14:53,440 --> 00:14:56,480
But do not mourn, dear girl, do not fear.

127
00:14:56,480 --> 00:15:04,320
I will proclaim, I will prove your innocence, I will melt the stony hearts of your enemies by the tears and prayers.

128
00:15:04,320 --> 00:15:05,920
You shall not die.

129
00:15:05,920 --> 00:15:11,520
You, my playfellow, my companion, my sister, perish on the scaffold?

130
00:15:11,520 --> 00:15:18,320
No, I could never survive so horrible a misfortune.

131
00:15:18,320 --> 00:15:20,960
Justine shook her head mournfully.

132
00:15:20,960 --> 00:15:23,920
I do not fear to die, she said.

133
00:15:23,920 --> 00:15:26,000
That pang is past.

134
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:30,080
God raises my weakness and gives me courage to endure the worst.

135
00:15:30,080 --> 00:15:39,280
I leave a sad and bitter world, and if you remember me and think of me as of one unjustly condemned, I am resigned to the fate awaiting me.

136
00:15:39,280 --> 00:15:46,160
Learn from me, dear lady, to submit in patience to the will of heaven.

137
00:15:46,160 --> 00:15:54,160
During this conversation, I had retired to a corner of the prison room where I could conceal the horrid anguish that possessed me.

138
00:15:54,160 --> 00:15:55,200
Despair?

139
00:15:55,200 --> 00:15:57,040
Who dared talk of that?

140
00:15:57,280 --> 00:16:06,760
The poor victim, who on the morrow was to pass the awful boundary between life and death, felt not as I did, such deep and bitter agony.

141
00:16:06,760 --> 00:16:13,640
I gnashed my teeth and ground them together, uttering a groan that came from my inmost soul.

142
00:16:13,640 --> 00:16:15,800
Justine started.

143
00:16:15,800 --> 00:16:22,520
When she saw who it was, she approached me and said, Dear sir, you are very kind to visit me.

144
00:16:22,520 --> 00:16:27,240
I do hope you do not believe that I am guilty.

145
00:16:27,240 --> 00:16:29,160
I could not answer.

146
00:16:29,160 --> 00:16:31,400
No, Justine said Elizabeth.

147
00:16:31,400 --> 00:16:38,600
He is more convinced of your innocence than I was, for even when he heard that you had confessed, he did not credit it.

148
00:16:38,600 --> 00:16:40,440
I truly thank him.

149
00:16:40,440 --> 00:16:46,920
In these last moments, I feel the sincerest gratitude towards those who think of me with kindness.

150
00:16:46,920 --> 00:16:50,840
How sweet is the affection of others to such a wretch as I am.

151
00:16:50,840 --> 00:17:03,480
It removes more than half my misfortune, and I feel as if I could die in peace now that my innocence is acknowledged by you, dear lady, and your cousin.

152
00:17:03,480 --> 00:17:08,200
Thus the poor sufferer tried to comfort others and herself.

153
00:17:08,200 --> 00:17:21,240
She indeed gained the resignation she desired, but I, the true murderer, felt the never-dying worm alive in my bosom, which allowed of no hope or consolation.

154
00:17:21,240 --> 00:17:34,120
Elizabeth also wept and was unhappy, but hers also was the misery of innocence, which, like a cloud that passes over the fair moon, for a while hides but cannot tarnish its brightness.

155
00:17:34,120 --> 00:17:38,040
Anguish and despair had penetrated into the core of my heart.

156
00:17:38,040 --> 00:17:42,360
I bore a hell within me, which nothing could extinguish.

157
00:17:42,360 --> 00:17:48,720
We stayed several hours with Justine, and it was with great difficulty that Elizabeth could tear herself away.

158
00:17:48,960 --> 00:17:52,720
I wish, cried she, that I were to die with you.

159
00:17:52,720 --> 00:17:56,720
I cannot live in this world of misery.

160
00:17:56,720 --> 00:18:03,280
Justine assumed an air of cheerfulness, while she with difficulty repressed her bitter tears.

161
00:18:03,280 --> 00:18:13,440
She embraced Elizabeth and said in a voice of half-suppressed emotion, Farewell, sweet lady, dearest Elizabeth, my beloved and only friend.

162
00:18:13,440 --> 00:18:17,760
May heaven, in its bounty, bless and preserve you.

163
00:18:17,760 --> 00:18:21,360
May this be the last misfortune that you will ever suffer.

164
00:18:21,360 --> 00:18:27,600
Live and be happy and make others so.

165
00:18:59,520 --> 00:19:04,480
She perished on the scaffold as a murderess.

166
00:19:04,480 --> 00:19:11,200
From the tortures of my own heart, I turned to contemplate the deep and voiceless grief of my Elizabeth.

167
00:19:11,200 --> 00:19:21,440
This also was my doing, and my father's woe and the desolation of that late so smiling home, all was the work of my thrice-accursed hands.

168
00:19:21,440 --> 00:19:25,920
Ye weep, unhappy ones, but these are not your last tears.

169
00:19:25,920 --> 00:19:32,600
Again shall you rise the funeral wail, and the sound of your lamentations shall again and again be heard.

170
00:19:32,920 --> 00:19:51,960
Frankenstein, your son, your kinsman, your early much loved friend, he who would spend each vital drop of blood for your sakes, who has no thought nor sense of joy, except as it is mirrored also in your dear countenances, who would fill the air with blessings and spend his life in serving you.

171
00:19:51,960 --> 00:19:55,880
He bids you weep to shed countless tears.

172
00:19:55,880 --> 00:20:07,400
Happy beyond his hopes, if thus inexorable fate be satisfied, and if the destruction pause before the peace of the grave have succeeded to your sad torment.

173
00:20:07,400 --> 00:20:24,760
Thus spoke my prophetic soul, as torn by remorse, horror, and despair, I beheld those I loved spend vain sorrow upon the graves of William and Justine, the first hapless victims to my unhallowed arts.

174
00:20:24,760 --> 00:20:26,600
End of chapter 8.

175
00:20:26,600 --> 00:20:34,280
